#!/bin/bash
#PBS -N cgm_push
#PBS -P 11704243
#PBS -j oe
#   qsub -l select=1:ncpus=8:mem=32gb -l walltime=00:30:00 \
#        -o logs/98_push_report.log scripts/pbs/98_push_report.pbs            # -> qdev
source "${REPO:-$HOME/workspace/Project_CGM/CGM-OutlierMIA-master}/scripts/pbs/env.sh"
set +e
module load git/2.39.2
cd "$REPO"
{
  git add -A
  echo "staged: $(git diff --cached --numstat | wc -l) files"
  git commit -q -F - <<'MSG'
One standard: h256, 100,000 steps. Everything else was noise I introduced

The study had drifted into cells that differed in two or three things at once --
h96/h128/h256 crossed with 22k/40k/80k/100k steps crossed with different cohorts. Asked
to compare "one-day h128 100k" against "one-day h256 22k", there is no answer. That was
mine, and it came from optimising each run's cost in isolation.

From here there is one standard and two variables:

                     1 channel (CGM)     2 channels (CGM + basal/kg)
    1 day            matrix_d1_c1        matrix_d1_c2
    7 days           matrix_d7_c1        matrix_d7_c2

All at hidden_size 256 and 100,000 steps, all on the SAME 506 subjects, all using ONE
design. A subject qualifies only with complete days, a basal trace with a recorded body
mass, and seven consecutive complete days; the three-way intersection is 534, and the
seven-day two-channel cell then returns 506 because a day needs all 288 cells of EVERY
channel, which leaves fewer unbroken weeks. The matrix runs on what all four cells
share. The outlier consensus is re-derived once on the one-day CGM view and reused
unchanged, so "who counts as an outlier" cannot vary with the channel set.

Chained with afterany rather than sized against a walltime estimate: loo.train skips a
model that already has samples.npy, so a chain finishes a cell however long each model
takes. That bet has been lost twice and will not be made a third time.
~1,496 GPU.h, critical path the seven-day cells at ~138 h.

WHAT THE DISCARDED WORK ESTABLISHED, before it was superseded:

- Two-channel at 80,000 steps reads max 0.878, spread 0.373 -- still bimodal, median
  worse than 40k's. Doubling the budget does nothing, so cutting to 40k was not what
  broke it. Width or the basal/kg channel remain.
- The one-day control at the seven-day training regime reads arm AUC 0.940 against the
  seven-day 0.950. At a fixed T=288, going from 36 epochs to 201 moves the arm AUC from
  0.680 to 0.940. Window length was worth 0.010.
- The within-arm reading, which had not been reported and should have been: the CONTROL
  arm never moves -- per-subject mean 0.5018 / 0.4863 / 0.4594 / 0.4994 across every
  condition, and 0 of 10 controls above 0.55 in any of them. The OUTLIER arm does:
  0.5129 at 36 epochs (1 of 20 above 0.55), 0.575-0.628 at 201 (6 of 10). The risk
  tracks how often the generator sees each training window, and it lands on outliers.

A LATENT TRAP FOUND ON THE WAY. seed_stability defaulted its clinical-metrics cache to
results/outliers/_clinical.parquet -- built 2026-08-06 on metabonet875, keyed on the old
bare `id`. It hard-links that into any cohort's run. Here it failed loudly with a
KeyError; on a cohort sharing the key SCHEME it would have silently scored one cohort's
subjects with another's metrics. Default is now local to the run, and outliers/run.py
refuses a cache that does not cover its subjects instead of raising a pandas KeyError.

Also: docs/report/lab.html, a lab diary whose spine is MEMO's decision chain -- 20
entries, each with the observation, the reading and what it forced, cross-linked to the
day view and the report. Laid out without a graph library: the chain's layers are the
chronology and each holds one node, so d3-dag's Sugiyama solver would have nothing to
solve and would cost ~300 KB in a file that has to stay self-contained.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
MSG
  git log --oneline -1
  git -c credential.helper="store --file=$HOME/.git-credentials-github" \
      push origin main 2>&1 | sed -E 's#//[^@]*@#//***@#g'
  echo "push exit ${PIPESTATUS[0]}"
} 2>&1 | tee logs/98_push_report.live.log
